_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8238b0bd-1624-4cbd-8a34-fc44254ec8eb_1080x1080.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!GMsm!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8238b0bd-1624-4cbd-8a34-fc44254ec8eb_1080x1080.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!GMsm!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8238b0bd-1624-4cbd-8a34-fc44254ec8eb_1080x1080.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!GMsm!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8238b0bd-1624-4cbd-8a34-fc44254ec8eb_1080x1080.jpeg 1456w\" sizes=\"100vw\" loading=\"lazy\"></picture><div class=\"image-link-expand\"><div class=\"pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset\"><button tabindex=\"0\" type=\"button\" class=\"pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image buttonBase-GK1x3M\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" viewBox=\"0 0 20 20\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke=\"var(--color-fg-primary)\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" class=\"icon-noB79L\"><g><path d=\"M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882\"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex=\"0\" type=\"button\" class=\"pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image buttonBase-GK1x3M\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2 icon-noB79L\"><polyline points=\"15 3 21 3 21 9\"></polyline><polyline points=\"9 21 3 21 3 15\"></polyline><line x1=\"21\" x2=\"14\" y1=\"3\" y2=\"10\"></line><line x1=\"3\" x2=\"10\" y1=\"21\" y2=\"14\"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><h4><strong>Free Online Intro Session</strong></h4><p>Register your interest here to join our free online introductory session to learn about the framework that we will be sharing during the program.</p><p class=\"button-wrapper\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://beingme.com.sg/power-and-pause&quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;Intro Session Details&quot;,&quot;action&quot;:null,&quot;class&quot;:&quot;button-wrapper&quot;}\" data-component-name=\"ButtonCreateButton\"><a class=\"button primary button-wrapper\" href=\"https://beingme.com.sg/power-and-pause\"><span>Intro Session Details</span></a></p><div><hr></div><p></p>",
  "body_text": "We often think of patience as something we simply have — or don’t have.\n\nTo us, it’s a personality trait, a virtue some people are born with, or something we tell ourselves to just be when things get difficult.\n\nBut khanti — patience as a quality of heart — is something far deeper than that.\n\nIt is the capacity to stay.\n\nTo stay when things don’t go your way.\n\nTo stay without collapsing, without retaliating, and without running away.\n\nThere is a teaching in the Buddhist tradition — a simile — that makes this viscerally clear. \n\nThe Buddha described a scenario in which bandits cut a person apart with a saw, bone by bone. Even then, he said, a good practitioner does not let hatred take over the heart. \n\nIt’s intentionally extreme, and we’re not meant to take it literally. That’s the nature of a simile — it stretches the imagination so the principle lands more deeply.\n\nThe point is this: no matter what comes at you, the heart can be protected.\n\nThink about our modern-day saws. The meeting that runs over, the WiFi dropping right when we need it most. The message we’ve tried to communicate ten different ways, and still no one seems to understand. The illness that isn’t healing as quickly as we would like.\n\nWhat are the saws in your life?\n\nThese moments of friction — small and large — are not obstacles to patience. They are the practice itself.\n\nThis is also a reframing of what it means to be strong.\n\nWe tend to associate strength with control: pushing through, winning, conquering. But khanti points us toward a different kind of strength — one that is deeply enduring.\n\nIt is the strength to remain present without being swept away.\n\nNot suppressing what we feel, not tolerating harm, but meeting reality as it is, without immediately adding to our suffering through reactivity.\n\nCan I stay with this moment without making it worse?\n\nThis simple question is the whole practice.\n\nKhanti asks something of us in three directions:\n\nPatience with discomfort. Life is full of things beyond our control. When we can’t immediately fix or escape what’s uncomfortable, we have a choice: to resist and suffer more, or to stay and meet it with some steadiness.\n\nPatience with others. This isn’t about putting up with people, or staying in situations that harm us. It’s about bringing more wisdom and compassion into how we respond — even when someone is difficult, even when we feel misunderstood. Kind speech is a form of patience. It takes real inner strength to soften the edges of our words when we’re frustrated.\n\nPatience with ourselves. This is perhaps the hardest one. We can be relentlessly harsh with ourselves — for not doing enough, not progressing fast enough, not being who we think we should be by now. Khanti invites us to trust a little more in the unfolding of our own path and becoming.\n\n---\n\nAt Take A Pause Live over 13 & 14 June 2026, we practiced the body scan as a way of cultivating patience.\n\nI won’t pretend the body scan is everyone’s favourite.\n\nI hear this often: “Please, not the body scan. It’s too slow, too long, too much.”\n\nAnd yet, that resistance is exactly why it’s so valuable for cultivating patience.\n\nThe body scan asks us to be with the body as it is — not to feel a certain way, not to reach a special state, but simply to notice what’s actually here. \n\nPleasant, unpleasant, neutral. Comfortable, uncomfortable. Whatever your reality is, that is what you’re meeting.\n\nAnd when the mind wanders — which it will — there will be that familiar flicker of impatience. Why can’t I focus? Why am I so restless? Why is this so hard?\n\nThose are the very moments khanti asks us to stay.\n\n---\n\nWe read the poem Wait by Galway Kinnell, and ended with some loving-kindness — a few quiet phrases offered to ourselves:\n\nMay I meet this moment with patience. \n\nMay I protect my heart from hatred and agitation. \n\nMay I breathe with this discomfort, one moment at a time. \n\nMay I remain steady when things don’t go my way.\n\n---\n\nMeditation Practice\n\nTap Play to Practice Together:\n\nThis post is free for 4 weeks, and then archived for paid subscribers to support a sustainable, long-term sharing of mindful content. Upgrade to receive lifetime access to the full archive and ongoing updates.\n\nShare\n\n---\n\nThe Wholehearted Woman Retreat\n\nA five-day mindfulness retreat into quiet wisdom and the light within\n\n1-5 October 2026\nSiem Reap, Cambodia\n\nLed by Erin Lee\n\nJoin Erin at Angkor Wat\n\n---\n\nPower & Pause Retreat\n\nA high-touch program supported by structured processes, guided practices, and reflective assignments\n\n24 - 28 September 2026\nUbud, Bali\n\nLed by Subba Sattvic and Erin Lee\n\nFree Online Intro Session\n\nRegister your interest here to join our free online introductory session to learn about the framework that we will be sharing during the program.\n\nIntro Session Details\n\n---",
